# External consumer independent review

Bounded production review: base 782309899fdc55aa9db05588f8cc0d9316ee8738, checkpoint 75e73a79022e238ac7355c37588c37b910759398, stdout/stderr correction 6507b146516eed172d78f4e9147ae3cc0994f284. Frozen files and deltas are retained. No shared source, test, spec, or Git edits by this reviewer. The final small test-only delta adds a fresh receiver and exact recovery code/stage assertions; its raw source and patch are captured separately.

The external package defines its own schema, reader configuration and Greeting form using four public foundation crates. Its workspace is explicitly independent; it does not import tools fixtures or private modules. The real metadata's complete packages list contains exactly nepl3-core, nepl3-reader, nepl3-engine and nepl3-wire at the expected canonical paths; workspace root, sole member, resolve root and consumer manifest agree with the actual OS temporary directory outside the repository. Foundation sources remain monorepo path dependencies with parent workspace metadata; this proves neither separately packaged foundation distribution nor independent SemVer/process-provider compatibility.

Independent actual tests copied the sample into a retained OS temporary workspace and a unique target directory, added explicit malformed ReadSpecId and final/nonfinal operand cases, and decoded canonical production wire into a separate codec with an empty SourceStore/admission. Native five and wasm32-wasip2 five tests passed. Original and updated test-delta runs retain commands, complete logs, source snapshots, native/wasm binary hashes, and precise target/version. Unicode name positions are bytes 6..12, source revision is 7, Direct Origin matches the name head, and final missing input/recovery, nonfinal Unicode/NeedMore, malformed NDF/wire and cancellation remain distinct. Setup and operation use finite sample budgets; this is not an exhaustive quota or continuation/provider test.

Three initial orchestration defects were independently reproduced before root fixes: a duplicate nepl3 package hidden by dictionary collapse; passed paired with a final changed-source fingerprint; and loss of timeout partial output/command. The unchanged initial runner and probe records remain. The fixed runner rejects duplicate row count before name/path comparison; computes pass only after a single terminal fingerprint; and records timeout command plus available stdout/stderr. Fixed fault probes reject all three. The fingerprint probe changes on the second/final fingerprint in the corrected implementation, because the third terminal fingerprint no longer exists; the original schedule is preserved in the before probe. Fault-injected subprocesses are not actual Cargo execution.

The root subsequently found a real initial-CI failure: dependency-download progress merged into metadata JSON. The two-file 6507b14 delta separates stdout/stderr with separate hashes and preserves timeout partial streams. All six managed orchestration tests passed independently. The actual production runner at 6507b14 also passed pinned rustc, format, exact metadata validation, Clippy and three native production API tests outside the repository. Its output streams and foundation before/after hashes were independently verified. The command started after the stderr correction, as confirmed by recorded commit and separate stderr receipt fields; the earlier 75 snapshot remains a historical source checkpoint.

CI executes the consumer on the native host matrix and uploads its evidence even on failure. This reviewer executed Windows native and Windows Wasmtime 44.0.1, not Linux/macOS hosts or a process provider. Stale output directories are rejected by exclusive mkdir; subprocess timeout is 600 seconds per command. A setup failure before record initialization may leave no result file, but exits unsuccessfully and is not a passed receipt. Source fingerprinting covers Git-tracked foundation files, not every possible environment/build input; the record is not a hermetic build attestation.

The new spec22/task/catalog boundaries were read directly. T26 is in-progress, X01/X02 remain not-run; the sample does not complete the extraction task. No runtime package/schema changes were introduced to make the sample pass. No remaining blocker was found in this first public-path-consumer slice. Independent-release compatibility, dynamic package/Profile loading, actual process lifecycle exchange, CI cross-host outcomes and formal acceptance remain unproven by this review.

The test-delta rerun reused the same isolated external target with unchanged foundation sources and changed only consumer tests. Its new binaries replace the original binaries at those paths; the earlier binary digests are capture-time evidence, not a claim that those old executable bytes remain on disk. Both original and final consumer source/logs are retained, and the final binary hashes are in test-delta/results.json.

Archive scope: the initial recursive fixture capture unintentionally included an existing author target directory. The original initial manifest is preserved without rewriting, but its target cache entries are explicitly excluded from this final source/log evidence manifest (archive-scope.json). None of those cached artifacts were used for the independent unique-target executions. Original cache bytes remain local; this is not a fully archived build-cache reproduction claim.
